<!-- awf:section preamble -->
# {{ .prefix }}-brainstorming

Brainstorming owns clarification, design exploration, proportionate presentation, conditional grounding invocation, and final grounded-design approval. It does not create an effort or require one.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Use only when behavior, scope, structure, dependencies, patterns, checks, or testing need a material choice or clarification. Proceed directly when the intended outcome, solution shape, affected boundary, and verification are already clear.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure -->
## Procedure

1. **Orient in the topic.** Invoke `{{ .prefix }}-orienting` when its repository-truth moments apply, then run `awf context <relevant paths>`.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
2. **Clarify one question at a time.** Narrow the outcome without inventing requirements.
3. **Present proportionate approaches.** Offer alternatives with trade-offs and a recommendation when a material choice exists; a straightforward choice may need only a concise comparison.
4. **Evaluate continuity independently.** Invoke `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` only when durable continuity materially helps. Brainstorming itself never creates an effort, and design may remain effort-free.
5. **Evaluate repository-premise risk independently.** If correctness depends on broad or uncertain repository facts, hidden coupling, cross-domain effects, unfamiliar architecture, or high-consequence assumptions, invoke `{{ .prefix }}-grounding`. A localized design choice may skip grounding, while a clear broad-repository design may ground without further brainstorming. Resolve its mechanical, reasoned, or user-decision findings in this workflow; do not dispatch the grounding checker directly or loop grounding.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section example-clarifying-questions -->
Ask focused questions one at a time and avoid inventing requirements. Prefer questions that expose the actual behavior, boundary, trade-off, or verification choice.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section design-sections -->
Per `docs/maintainable-code-design.md`, settle the semantic model and ownership, representation boundaries, dependency direction, test seams, and preparatory-refactor decision in a proportionate simplicity contract: scope and exclusions, structural approach and dependencies, patterns or abstractions, and checks and testing strategy. Scale the presentation to the choice; a straightforward change may settle these in a few sentences rather than a fixed checklist. The final approved design becomes the implementation boundary.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section no-spec-rule -->
Do not create a separate specification. Put a choice in an ADR only when it remains load-bearing after implementation or changes an active current-state claim. Write a plan only when sequencing, coordination, or resumability materially helps. Neither artifact follows automatically from brainstorming or from the other artifact.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
## Final approval

Present the complete design after any grounding findings are resolved, explicitly request approval, and stop. If the user requests changes, revise and re-present without recursively grounding unless changed facts newly warrant it. Once approved, independently evaluate the ADR, plan, continuity, and implementation-review triggers; no later mechanism is automatic.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-approval -->
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section definitions -->
A load-bearing decision remains meaningful after implementation.{{ with .data.loadBearingExamples }} Examples:{{ range . }} {{ .item }};{{ end }}{{ end }} A plan is warranted only when sequencing, coordination, or resumability materially helps.{{ with .data.errorBoundaries }} Consider configured error boundaries:{{ range . }} {{ .name }};{{ end }}{{ end }}
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section anti-patterns -->
Do not use line count, artifact type, another mechanism firing, or a bundled "non-trivial" label as a trigger. Do not skip explicit final approval when brainstorming fires.
<!-- awf:end -->
